+the text of every state to the role's label: the release's `color-contrast` pick against the role's
+fill in each mode, which § Semantic roles describes. Every role takes the white label in both
+modes, apart from the `light` role, which takes the black label in both, and the dark primary,
+which takes the black label because white reads about 2.6 to 1 on its light cyan. Hover and active
+backgrounds mix the fill at the `--vn-state-hover` and `--vn-state-active` weights toward the
+endpoint that leads away from the label, so the label's contrast rises from rest to hover to active. A fill under
+the white label shades toward the light `--vn-state-mixer` value, Elements' measured near-black,
+and a fill under the black label tints toward the `--vn-palette-white-base` token. The `light` and `dark`
+roles move toward their label instead, as the release's button loop moves them, because each fill
+is an end of the gray ramp and a move past that end shows nothing: the `light` fill shades and the
+`dark` fill tints. The dark primary therefore lightens when hovered and pressed, as the release's
+own `info` and `warning` fills do. The border keeps the resting fill. Outline roles use the role
+color for resting text and border over a transparent background, fill on hover with the label over
+it, and take the active mix while pressed, a checked outline included. Their disabled state
+restores the transparent surface and role text. The ring remains primary for every role. Only the
+bare `button` rule and the bare `.btn` hover and active backgrounds read the `--vn-state-mixer`
+token, as the veil over a transparent ground.
+
+The styles build computes each label and each direction from the role's triplet, so a runtime
+retune of a role's fill keeps the compiled label and direction. A retune that moves a fill across
+the 4.5 to 1 boundary for white also sets the `--bs-btn-color`, `--bs-btn-hover-color`,
+`--bs-btn-active-color`, `--bs-btn-disabled-color`, `--bs-btn-hover-bg`, and `--bs-btn-active-bg`
+variables on the classes it retunes. The `tests/src/styles/components/button.test.ts` proof reads the label
+of every filled and outline state against the 4.5 to 1 bar in each mode, and the direction each
+state fill and the label's contrast move.
 
